<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	This app is deliberately NOT App Sandboxed (PROJECT-SPEC.md section 12:
	personal local build, no App Store distribution). Because there is no
	sandbox, entitlements below are limited to what the hardened runtime
	(`--options runtime`, required for codesign to even accept an
	entitlements file, and good practice even for a personal build) would
	otherwise block. Nothing is requested "just in case" — each key is
	justified against a concrete, evidenced need.
	-->

	<!-- REQUIRED: .NET's JIT (RyuJIT) writes executable machine code into
	     memory at run time. Under the hardened runtime this is blocked
	     unless the process holds allow-jit. Without it, the self-contained
	     .NET 10 apphost fails to start (or falls back to a crippled
	     interpreter-only mode) the moment it needs to JIT anything, which
	     is immediately at startup. This is the one entitlement every
	     hardened-runtime .NET app needs. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	The following two entitlements were considered and are DELIBERATELY
	OMITTED:

	- com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory: this permits
	  running memory that was never signed at all (e.g. a JIT that writes
	  code without going through Apple's JIT APIs, or old-style
	  self-modifying code). .NET's JIT on Apple Silicon uses the standard
	  mmap(MAP_JIT)/pthread_jit_write_protect_np pattern together with
	  allow-jit above; it does not need this broader escape hatch, and
	  granting it would defeat a real part of the hardened-runtime code
	  integrity guarantee for no evidenced benefit.

	- com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: this permits
	  loading dylibs signed by a different Team ID than the main
	  executable. ShareX-Mac's only bundled native dependency,
	  libShareXMacNative.dylib, is signed in the SAME build with the SAME
	  local identity ("ShareX-Mac Local") as the app itself (see
	  scripts/build-app.sh, which signs the dylib before the outer bundle).
	  Library validation only rejects dylibs signed by a *different*
	  identity/Team ID, or unsigned ones; a same-identity, signed dylib
	  loads fine with library validation left ON. This would only be
	  needed if ShareX-Mac loaded a third-party unsigned/differently-signed
	  plugin dylib at runtime, which it does not.
	-->

	<!-- REQUIRED: screen recording and window capture go through
	     ScreenCaptureKit (see native/ShareXMacNative/swift/SXMCapture.swift),
	     which is gated by the system Screen Recording TCC permission, not
	     an entitlement. No screen-recording-specific entitlement exists to
	     request here; the com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit entitlement above
	     and the Info.plist NSMicrophoneUsageDescription key are what this
	     app actually needs declared. -->
</dict>
</plist>
